      "native_complex_comment": "Deoxythymidine kinase catalyzes the ATP-dependent conversion of deoxythymidine to dTMP and is an enzyme of the <a href=\"http://biocyc.org/ECOLI/new-image?object=PWY0-181\">salvage pathways of pyrimidine deoxyribonucleotides</a> [<a href=\"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/4228236\">Hiraga67</a>].\n<p>\nBiochemical analysis of thymidine kinase and its allosteric regulation was largely performed with the <i>E. coli</i> B enzyme.  Early studies indicated that the enzyme dimerizes in the presence of an activator or an inhibitor deoxynucleotide [<a href=\"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/4861610\">Iwatsuki67</a>]; in the absence of regulatory nucleotides, temperature affects the affinity of the enzyme for its substrates [<a href=\"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/4861611\">Iwatsuki67a</a>].  \n<p>\nGel filtration experiments with crude extracts resulted in a native molecular weight of 82 kDa, suggesting that the <i>E. coli</i> K-12 enzyme also forms a homomultimeric complex [<a href=\"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/2041474\">Black91</a>].\n<p>\nMutants resistant to fluorodeoxyuridine (FUdR) are deficient in thymidine kinase activity [<a href=\"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/8376351\">Summers93</a>].  A <i>tdk</i> deletion mutant grows normally, but is RecA-dependent [<a href=\"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/15531636\">Kouzminova04</a>].  A <i>tdk</i> deletion and the <i>dut-1</i> allele [<a href=\"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/18586941\">Ting08</a>] as well as <i>tdk</i> and <i>ndk</i> [<a href=\"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/18621712\">Nordman08</a>] are synthetically lethal.\n<p>\nTdk: \"deoxy<b>t</b>hymi<b>d</b>ine <b>k</b>inase\" [<a href=\"http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/4868676\">Igarashi67</a>]",
      "component_uniprot_xrefs": [
        "P23331"
      ]
    }
